A: First, ensure you add a reference to the PexLib as it is a common library that provides contracts and is usually included in the test projects.
Second, you could use PexAssumeForAll to ensure your assumptions are correct. For instance:
[PexMethod]
[PexAssumeForAll(typeof(ArgumentException))]
public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeNotNull] ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));

    // Test the method.
}

This way, if the ArrayList does not contain x, the PexRunner will skip the test.
Regarding the "@@@ If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list" comment - it's hard to test for this condition as it's impossible to get old position without the old list, as lists are not persistent data structures. You would have to save the initial positions when adding elements to the ArrayList, and then compare the old positions with the new positions in the array.
The assertion for this would then look something like:
Assert.IsTrue(New_ret >= oldPositionOfX);

This would be a conditional contract that makes sure that the return value is non-negative and is more than or equal to old position of the item. Note that oldPositionOfX is not defined in your original post so please define it as needed.
Lastly, I would advise against using ArrayList and List as they are obsolete. Consider using List<T> instead, which is more type safe and prevents index out of range exceptions.

A: Here is how you can write the contract to test the LastIndexOf method of ArrayList.


*

*Before and After adding the elements, test whether the item is in the list.

*Check whether the lastIndex returned by the method is valid (greater than or equal to 0 and less than the total number of elements in list).

*Test whether, if the item is present in the list multiple times, then LastIndexOf returns an appropriate index that is greater than or equal to the index of the first occurrence of the item in the list.


Here is the contract:
[PexMethod]
[PexAssumeNotNull("*")]
public void LastIndexOfTest([PexAssumeNotNull]ArrayList list, [PexAssumeNotNull]object value)
{
    // Initial setup
    int count = list.Count;

    // Add value to list
    list.Add(value);

    // Assertion - after adding, value must be in list
    Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(value));

    // Invoke method under test
    int index = ((IList)list).LastIndexOf(value);

    // Assertions
    // count should be equal to or greater than index
    Assert.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < count);
    // if item is present multiple times, index should be greater or equal to the earliest occurrence of the item in the list.
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
    {
        if (list[i].Equals(value))
        {
            Assert.IsTrue(index >= i);
            break;
        }
    }
}

I've made some assumptions about the signature of the method. If you know the method signature is different, please specify the contract accordingly.
Please note that testing last index requires additional logic compared to other Pex tests because you need to manipulate the item array before and after the add method to generate the expected results.
You may want to modify or disable certain tests based on the array bounds. For example, if the array is full, adding an element should throw a NotSupportedException. Pex can't currently generate tests for this scenario. Consider using the Non-deterministic contract attribute to disable tests on full capacity lists.
Also, Pex might generate tests where the List class's Count property is called more than once for the same List instance. You may want to consider filtering methods based on the PexCurrentValues and PexHasExited property.
Lastly, since Pex only works with public methods, ensure that your methods are public or make them public if they are private or protected for Pex to detect them.
Consider changing to the System.Collections.Generic.List<T>. It provides the same functionality as ArrayList but with improved type-safety.
