A: Since the List.LastIndexOf() method is not considered part of the Microsoft.Contracts framework, you cannot use PexContract and its associated attributes to test it.
Instead you would need to explicitly perform your tests with the correct inputs and compare the results with the expected results to check if you're method is working as expected.
You need to create input for multiple cases covering all corner conditions - like when you do not have such element in the list etc. 
One more thing is that since you are dealing with ArrayList, ArrayList.LastIndexOf() method returns a type of int, which means if it doesn't find anything, it returns -1.
So, using that knowledge you can create test cases and observe the result.
Here is a simple example of what Pex test method for last index of should look like:
[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]
[DataTestMethod]
[PexMethod]
public void TestLastIndexOf([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid]int searchElement)
{
    int expectedResult = arrList.LastIndexOf(searchElement); // this is the expected result from original function

    // actual function call
    int actualResult = ArrayList.LastIndexOf(arrList, searchElement); // this is your function call

    // comparison
    Assert.AreEqual(expectedResult, actualResult);
} 

I'm assuming you are running this as a unit test in MS Test. If you are using NUnit or xUnit, you can replace [PexMethod] with [Test], [HostType("Moles")] with nothing and [DataTestMethod] with [DataRow] (you will need to populate that by hand, because Pex won't be able to find possible inputs automatically).
Remember to take care of the possible empty list scenarios too. If you are only checking the positive scenarios, what happens when the list is empty? This is something you will have to think about.
Lastly, ensure you thoroughly test your case such as when search element is not in the list and when element is at last position in list etc. to make sure you are thoroughly covering all the bases.

A: While Pex can create unit tests, it does not currently provide support for contract-based or postcondition-based testing for methods not included in the Contract Framework. In this case, ArrayList.LastIndexOf method.
Potential workarounds could include manually creating your own tests or utilising other tools capable of performing such tasks, such as Moles or a combination of ReSharper's test generator capability and manual refinement.
Also, the contract is not specified for LastIndexOf method in the reference source so it could be possible that the method is not included in the framework. You would need to rely on other tests or documentation to validate its behavior.
It would be worth raising this with the Microsoft Pex team to have this added into the framework.
Q: Get the value of a field that is not an input field in Vue.js I have a Vue.js application where I need to get the value of a field that doesn't exist in the DOM like the value of a computed field. Is it possible to get the value of this field?

A: Yes, you can. Vue.js computes model properties and listens to the DOM for changes that could possibly cause a model to update. So, you can create a data property as a computed property that holds this value and use it in place of the actual value in the template. 
Here's an example for the given case:
<template>
    <div>
        {{myProperty}}
    </div>
</template>

<script>
    export default {
        data() {
            return {
                notExistingFieldValue: 'Default value', //initialize it 
            }
        },
        computed: {
            myProperty() {
                return this.notExistingFieldValue;
            }
        },
        mounted(){
          // assuming we know the condition needed for changing this.notExistingFieldValue
          // If you don't know the condition, you might implement it differently
          // This is an example for changing once the component is mounted
          this.notExistingFieldValue = 'New value';
        }
    }
</script--------------------
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret == -1 ? Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1 : New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);

            PexAssume.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX);
        } 

        [PexMethod]
        [PexEntryPoint]
        public void Main()
        {
            var input = new PexMethod<ArrayList>(new object[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 });
            PexTestCase[] testCases = PexRunner.ExploreStateBoundary(input);

            foreach (var testCase in testCases)
            {
                try
                {
                    PexExecution.Run(testCase);
                }
                catch (Exception e)
                {
                    ReportIssue("ArrayList.LastIndexOf test case {0} failed with: {1}", testCase.Id, e);
                }
            }
        }

        private void ReportIssue(string message, object arg0, Exception e)
        {
            if (!(e is PexException))
            {
                System.Diagnostics.Trace.WriteLine(string.Format(message, arg0, e));
                // other ways to report the issue depend on your testing framework.
                // for instance, in MStest, you'd typically call Assert.Fail(message, e);
            }
        }
   } 
}


This method exploits state boundary rules which have been described here: http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/proj/stateexplore/
So, it explores all possible combination of adding x and removing other elements, and then checks that the last index of x is consistent with what the user of the list has expected.

It also performs exploratory testing of state boundaries for LastIndexOf (the same way as for Add). It would go through all possible arrays of possible state and pick the one which would cause some problem in the code, like in your case.
This is what exploratory testing about finding interesting cases and possible problems about your code. It also will give you the confidence that your program operates correctly at the state of exploration.
In a way, exploratory testing finds a corner case that could have led to a bug if this test case was not there.

This sample covers Pex's state-based testing. More sample can be found directly from Pex Studio under "test explorer". Including how to use PexObserve to observe the value, PexAssume and PexAssert for assumptions and assertions etc.
Q: Is it possible to force my custom directive to always have a parent container with a specific class? I found a directive on this stackoverflow post: 
.directive('makeEditable', function() {    
    var link = function(scope, element, attrs) {
        element.css('cursor','pointer');     
        element.wrap("<div class='editable'></div>");
        element.parent().click(function(){
            element.text('').focus();
        });
        element.blur(function() {
             element.parent().html(element.text());
        });      
};  
return {
    restrict: "C", 
    link: link
}
});

However, I need to ensure that my custom directive (makeEditable) will always have a parent container with a specific class .editable. Is there a way to achieve this, or do I have to manually ensure the parent container contains this class name?

A: Instead of using .wrap() function or .addClass() (which both can't be controlled by you to ensure that there's always a certain parent class), you can use the template function of the directive to control how your directive behave:
app.directive("makeEditable", function() {    
    return {
        restrict: "C", 
        template: "<div class='editable'><span ng-transclude></span></div>",
        transclude:true
}
});

Here's an example of how you can use it:
<div make-editable>Text</div>

This will output:
<div class="editable"><span>Text</span></div>

This way you never lose the control over the parent div and it will always have the class .editable. The ng-transclude part will make sure the content inside your make-editable div will be inserted--------------------	    Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}

//TestDriven/Tests/ArrayList/Test/ArrayListTests.cs
﻿using System;
using NUnit.Framework;
using System.Collections;
using Android.Util;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture]
    public class ArrayListTests
    {
        [Test]
        public void RemoveAt_ArrayList_Success()
        {
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
            arrList.Add(1);
            arrList.Add(2);
            arrList.Add(3);
            arrList.Add(4);
            arrList.RemoveAt(1); // Remove 2;

            Assert.AreEqual(3, arrList.Count);
            Assert.IsFalse(arrList.Contains(2));
        }
    
        [Test]
        public void Insert_ArrayList_Success()
        {
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
            arrList.Add(1);
            arrList.Add(3);
            arrList.Add(4);
            arrList.Insert(1, 2); // Insert 2 at index 1;

            Assert.AreEqual(4, arrList.Count);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(2));
            Assert.AreEqual(2, arrList[1]);
        }

        [Test]
        public void Clear_ArrayList_Success()
        {
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
            arrList.Add(1);
            arrList.Add(2);
            arrList.Add(3);
            arrList.Clear();

            Assert.AreEqual(0, arrList.Count);
        }
    }
}

